note: |
  BLIND-EVALUATION SNAPSHOT — VALUE TIER. NOT a publishable artifact and NOT a version of Ed-Fi.
  Must never reach a golden. Purpose: measure the inference bridge's VALUE-TIER judgment against
  Ed-Fi's own authored option-code crosswalk without letting the bridge see the answer.

  Construction (mechanical, makeValueSnapshot03.py in dataStores/blindEval/):
  1. The 8,310 descriptor rows dedup to 3,104 unique (descriptorName, codeValue) option values,
     mirroring the parser's canonical dedup. Representative row per value = first occurrence
     carrying a CEDSOptionCode (parser merge mirrored), else the first occurrence.
  2. Sorted by (descriptorName, codeValue), every 31st taken -> 101 values. No hand-picking.
  3. BLINDED at the source: descriptor columns 9-17 are EMPTY — the six CEDS* columns per
     snapshot 02's rule, PLUS OptionSetMatchConfidence, ElementMatchConfidence, and Notes
     (supervisor ruling 2026-08-03: a grading column in the blinded file is itself a leak; the
     parser provably emits none of the three into node properties, this is belt-and-suspenders).
  4. The elements file carries ONE row per sampled descriptor (the first snapshot-01 row whose
     Descriptor-typed element name matches), every CEDS* column blanked, so each option set gets
     its constraining field and owning entity. Descriptors with no matching elements row are
     orphan-anchored by the forge (by design).

  The withheld answers live in dataStores/blindEval/valueAnswerKey.json, read from snapshot 01.
